TP.register({id:"lp7-g4",lp:7,grade:"4",mode:"curriculum",checks:6,nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Eight O'Clock";const FLU=["The Westmill garden has two water barrels, one at each end of the beds, and the person on the rota fills them first. It takes about eleven minutes with the hose, which is long enough to walk the rows and see what the week did.","On the first Saturday in May, the gate was unlocked at two minutes past eight. The barrels were already running. Mr. Ferreira had come early, the way he does, and he did not say so.","By half past, nine people were working. Two were weeding bed four. One was tying up the peas, which had grown sideways in the wind and needed a second stake. Somebody's grandmother sat on the low wall and sorted seed packets into a shoebox by month.","Nobody gave orders. The job list was taped to the gate, and people crossed off what they finished. When a job got crossed off twice, which happened almost every week, the second person wrote a new job underneath."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="Saturday Seven";const STORY=["The Westmill garden runs on a paper rota taped inside the shed door. Seven names, seven Saturdays, and whoever is on the list opens the gate at eight.","Rosalind had signed up for the first Saturday in May, which was the one morning she did not want. Her cousin's band was playing at the library at nine, and she had been counting down to it for a month.","On Friday she found Mr. Ferreira in the shed, taping the rota back up where it had come loose.","“I can swap,” she said. “If anybody wants to.”","Mr. Ferreira read the list the way he reads everything, slowly, from the top. “Dev is on the twelfth,” he said. “Ask him. He owes me one after the mulch.” He almost smiled. “That mulch was a Herculean job. Nine wheelbarrows, by myself, while he was at the dentist.”","Dev said yes before she finished asking. He said the twelfth was nothing to him, his whole May was a blank page, and he would take the first Saturday and the twelfth both if she wanted.","“Just the first,” Rosalind said. “One for one.”","She wrote the change on the rota in pencil and put her name on the twelfth. Then she stood there a second longer, because the shed smelled like old rope and tomato leaves and she had not expected to like that.","On the twelfth it rained from eight until noon. Rosalind opened the gate anyway, and Dev was already under the eave with two cups of cocoa, standing like he had been there a while.","“You did not have to come,” she said.","“Neither did you,” he said, and handed her one."].join("\n\n");const ROTA_TITLE="What a Rota Does";const ROTA_SRC="Source 1: “What a Rota Does,” by Imogen Park. Westmill Register, March 2026.";const ROTA=["A rota is a list of names and dates that says whose turn it is. Hospitals use them. Fire stations use them. So does the Westmill community garden, where a sheet of paper taped inside the shed door decides who opens the gate on Saturday.","The garden tried it the other way first. For two summers, anyone who felt like it came on Saturday morning. Some Saturdays twenty people showed up and got in each other's way. Some Saturdays nobody came at all, and the beds went four days without water.","The rota closed the gaps. Seven names go on the sheet at a time, one for each of the next seven Saturdays. The season runs from April to October, which is 28 Saturdays, so the sheet gets written out four times. A person on the list has one job: be there at eight and open the gate.","If the day does not work, that person finds a swap and writes it on the sheet in pencil. The pencil matters. Last season the garden counted 9 swaps out of 28 Saturdays, and not one Saturday went unopened.","A rota also spreads the work. Before the sheet, three people opened the gate on most Saturdays. Now seven people share the season, four Saturdays each.","What a rota cannot do is make anyone care. It only makes it easy to know what you promised, and easy to tell when you have not kept it."].join("\n\n");const HAND_SRC="Source 2: Westmill Community Garden Handbook, 2026 edition, page 4.";const HAND=["If your name is on the sheet for Saturday, you have four jobs. Open the gate at eight. Fill both barrels before you do anything else. Put the job list on the gate. Lock up when the last person leaves.","If you cannot make your Saturday, find a swap and write it on the sheet in pencil. Do not cross a name out in pen. The sheet is the only record the garden keeps."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["volunteer","community","neighborly","responsible","gratitude","uncomfortable","disagreement","generosity","interruption","reluctantly","arrangement","wheelbarrow"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.2rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const stackFig=inner=>`<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;align-items:center;margin-top:10px">${inner}</div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">${t}</p>`;const srcLine=t=>`<p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px;font-size:1rem">${t}</p>`;const rotaHtml=small=>F.passage(ROTA,ROTA_TITLE,!!small);const twoSources=()=>`<div class="texts"><article>${srcLine(ROTA_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${ROTA.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</div></article><article>${srcLine(HAND_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${HAND.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</div></article></div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;function noteBoxes(a,b){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:250px;min-height:100px;padding:8px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.3rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}</div>`}const pvTable=(caption,digits)=>F.table(["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones"],[digits],{caption});const SEED_SWAP=["Every February the Westmill garden holds a seed swap in the library basement. People bring whatever seed they saved the year before, in envelopes, in jam jars, in old film canisters, and they lay it all out on three long tables.","Nothing is sold. You take what you want, and if you brought nothing at all, you still take what you want. The rule the organizers repeat every year is that a seed is worth nothing sitting in a drawer. Last February 64 people came, and the tables were cleared by half past ten."].join(" ");function plotShapes(){const one=(pts,n,alt)=>group(F.poly(pts,{size:180,fill:"var(--sea-soft)",alt}),"Plot "+n);return`<div class="figure" style="gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;align-items:flex-end">`+one([[10,26],[90,26],[90,74],[10,74]],1,"a rectangle, with four square corners")+one([[28,24],[96,24],[72,76],[4,76]],2,"a slanted four sided plot with two pairs of parallel sides and no square corner")+one([[16,18],[16,82],[86,82]],3,"a triangle with a square corner at the bottom left")+one([[50,16],[90,82],[14,82]],4,"a triangle with no square corner and no parallel sides")+`</div>`}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Shed Door Words",short:"Shed Words",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:75,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into parts you already know, then put it back together.",desc:"reading multisyllable words using letter-sound, syllable, and word-part knowledge",key:["Read each word in order: volunteer, community, neighborly, responsible, gratitude, uncomfortable, disagreement, generosity, interruption, reluctantly, arrangement, wheelbarrow.","Watch for: prefixes un- and dis- (uncomfortable, disagreement), the inter- prefix (interruption), suffixes -ible, -ity, -tion, -ly, -ment (responsible, generosity, interruption, neighborly, reluctantly, arrangement), the compound wheelbarrow, and the eigh spelling in neighborly.","Watch for the unstressed middle syllables, which often sound like uh: com-MU-ni-ty, re-SPON-si-ble, gen-er-OS-i-ty. A student who stalls is usually trying to sound out the whole word at once instead of chunking it."]}]},{id:2,title:"Ten Times the Seed",short:"Ten Times",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Look at the charts. Answer out loud and say what each digit is worth.",desc:"recognizing that a digit in one place is worth ten times what the same digit is worth in the place to its right",html:()=>lead("The garden counts seed in grams and keeps the totals on a place value chart."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-start;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(pvTable("4,440 grams of bean seed",["4","4","4","0"]),"Chart A")}${group(pvTable("5,050 grams of pea seed",["5","0","5","0"]),"Chart B")}</div>`,qs:["Chart A. What is the 4 in the hundreds place worth? What is the 4 in the tens place worth?","How do those two values compare? Say it as a sentence with ten times in it.","Think about the number 2,200. What are the two 2s worth? How do they compare?","One bag weighs 7,000 grams and another weighs 700 grams. How many times greater is the 7 in 7,000 than the 7 in 700?","Chart B. The two 5s in 5,050 are not worth the same. What is each one worth, and how many times greater is the left one?","A number has a 6 in the thousands place. What is that 6 worth? What would a 6 in the hundreds place be worth?"],key:["The 4 in the hundreds place is worth 400. The 4 in the tens place is worth 40. Partial: one of the two values given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","400 is ten times 40. A digit is worth ten times what the same digit is worth one place to its right. Partial: naming ten times with no general rule given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","The left 2 is worth 2,000 and the right 2 is worth 200. 2,000 is ten times 200. Partial: the two values right with no comparison is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","Ten times greater. 7,000 is ten times 700, because the 7 sits one place further left. Partial: 6,300 more is a difference and not a comparison of times, so it does not count. (4.NBT.A.1)","The left 5 is worth 5,000 and the right 5 is worth 50. They are two places apart, so apply ten times twice: 50 to 500 is ten times, and 500 to 5,000 is ten times again. 5,000 is 100 times 50. Partial: ten times is approaching here, since the two 5s sit two places apart. (4.NBT.A.1)","6,000 in the thousands place and 600 in the hundreds place. 6,000 is ten times 600. Partial: one of the two values given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Read Eight O'Clock",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:125,dir:"Read the passage out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Winter grade 4 benchmark: about 120 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, winter 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 95, 10th percentile about 71.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for a pause at the commas in the long sentence about the peas, and for self-corrections that show the student is reading for meaning."]}]},{id:4,title:"What a Rota Does",short:"The Rota",parts:[{std:["RI.4.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud. Use details and examples from the article in every answer.",desc:"explaining what an informational text says and drawing inferences, using details and examples",html:()=>rotaHtml(true),qs:["What is a rota? Read me the words from the article.","How many Saturdays are in the garden's season? Read me the words that tell you.","What happened on the Saturdays when nobody came? Give the detail from the article.","The article never says that the old way was unfair to some people. Use details to explain why it was.","Why does the writer say the pencil matters? The article does not say it straight out. Use details and examples to explain."],key:["A list of names and dates that says whose turn it is. Reads “A rota is a list of names and dates that says whose turn it is.” (RI.4.1, explicit)","28. Reads “The season runs from April to October, which is 28 Saturdays.” (RI.4.1, explicit)","The beds went four days without water. Reads “Some Saturdays nobody came at all, and the beds went four days without water.” (RI.4.1, explicit with the detail)","Because the work piled onto a few people. Details: “Before the sheet, three people opened the gate on most Saturdays,” against “Now seven people share the season, four Saturdays each.” The article never calls it unfair, so the student has to put the two numbers together. (RI.4.1, inference with details)","Because pencil means the sheet is meant to be changed, and changing it is what keeps the promise working. Examples: a person who cannot make the day “finds a swap and writes it on the sheet in pencil,” and “Last season the garden counted 9 swaps out of 28 Saturdays, and not one Saturday went unopened.” The swaps are the reason no Saturday was missed. (RI.4.1, inference with examples)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Same Amount, New Pieces",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Use the bars for number 1. Answer the rest out loud and say how you know. You can write on your paper.",desc:"explaining and generating equivalent fractions using a visual model",html:()=>lead("The crew splits the same long bed two different ways. The two bars below are the same length, so they stand for the same bed."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.fracBar(3,2,{width:330}),"Bar A, thirds")}${group(F.fracBar(6,4,{width:330}),"Bar B, sixths")}</div>`,qs:["Look at Bar A and Bar B. Is 2/3 equal to 4/6? Use the picture to explain what happened to each third.","Write a fraction equal to 3/4 that has 12 as the bottom number. Show the multiplying.","Explain why 5/8 and 10/16 are the same amount.","Is 2/5 equal to 6/15? How do you know?","Write 6/10 as an equal fraction with a smaller bottom number.","Which of these are equal to 1/2? 3/6, 4/9, 5/10, 7/14."],key:["Yes, 2/3 = 4/6. Each third was cut into 2 equal pieces, so the 3 parts became 6 and the 2 shaded parts became 4. The bars are the same length and the shaded parts cover the same amount. As an equation, 2/3 = (2 × 2)/(3 × 2) = 4/6. Partial: saying yes with nothing said about what happened to each third is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","9/12. Multiply the top and the bottom by the same number: 3 × 3 = 9 and 4 × 3 = 12. Partial: 9/12 right with no multiplying shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Each eighth is cut into 2 equal pieces, which turns 8 parts into 16 and turns the 5 shaded parts into 10. Nothing is added or taken away. As an equation, 5/8 = (5 × 2)/(8 × 2) = 10/16. Partial: naming the doubling with nothing said about the pieces being smaller is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Yes. Multiply the top and the bottom of 2/5 by 3: 2 × 3 = 6 and 5 × 3 = 15, so 2/5 = 6/15. Partial: saying yes with no multiplier shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","3/5. Divide the top and the bottom by 2: 6 ÷ 2 = 3 and 10 ÷ 2 = 5. Partial: 3/5 right with no dividing shown is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","3/6, 5/10, and 7/14 are all equal to 1/2, because the bottom number is twice the top number each time. 4/9 is not, because half of 9 is 4 and a half, so 4/9 is a little less than 1/2. Partial: two of the three right, or all three right with nothing said about 4/9, is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Saturday Seven",short:"The Swap",parts:[{std:["RL.4.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use details and examples from the story in every answer.",desc:"explaining what a story says and drawing inferences, using details and examples",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why did Rosalind want to swap her Saturday? Give the details from the story.","Which Saturday did Rosalind end up working, and what was the weather? Read me the words.","Why does Mr. Ferreira say Dev owes him one? Give the detail.","Dev offered to take both Saturdays. Rosalind said, “Just the first. One for one.” What does that tell you about her? Use a detail.","The story never says Rosalind likes being at the garden. How do you know she does? Give two details."],key:["Her cousin's band was playing at the library at nine on that same morning, and she “had been counting down to it for a month.” (RL.4.1, explicit with details)","The twelfth, and it rained. Reads “On the twelfth it rained from eight until noon.” (RL.4.1, explicit)","Because Mr. Ferreira moved the mulch alone. Reads “Nine wheelbarrows, by myself, while he was at the dentist.” (RL.4.1, explicit with the detail)","She wants an even trade rather than a favor, so she takes exactly one of his Saturdays for one of hers. The detail is that she wrote her own name on the twelfth right after, in pencil, instead of letting Dev cover both. (RL.4.1, inference with a detail)","She does, backed by two details: she “stood there a second longer, because the shed smelled like old rope and tomato leaves and she had not expected to like that,” and on the twelfth she “opened the gate anyway” in four hours of rain. (RL.4.1, inference with details)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Which Row Is Further Along?",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.2"],dur:140,dir:"Answer out loud and say how you know. You can write on your paper.",desc:"comparing two fractions with different numerators and denominators, and knowing when a comparison is valid",html:()=>lead("Everyone reports how far along their row is as a fraction. The rows on this stop are all the same length unless a question says otherwise."),fig:()=>stackFig(group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:1/8,hideNumbers:true,ticks:[[0,"0"],[.5,"1/2"],[1,"1"]],width:560}),"A line from 0 to 1 cut into eighths")+group(F.fracBar(12,7,{width:360}),"Twelfths, with 7 shaded")),qs:["Which is greater, 3/5 of a row or 5/8 of a row?","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 2/3 ____ 5/6","Look at the twelfths bar. Is 7/12 more or less than 1/2? Use the picture or a benchmark to explain.","Put these in order from least to greatest: 1/3, 3/8, 1/4.","Dev says 3/4 of his row is weeded. Rosalind says 2/3 of her row is weeded. Her row is twice as long as his. Can you tell whose row has more weeded ground from the fractions alone? Explain."],key:["5/8. In fortieths, 3/5 is 24/40 and 5/8 is 25/40, so 5/8 is greater by 1/40. Accept any correct common denominator or a clear benchmark argument. Partial: naming 5/8 with no common denominator and no benchmark is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","2/3 &lt; 5/6. In sixths, 2/3 is 4/6, and 4/6 is less than 5/6. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","More than 1/2. Half of 12 is 6, so 6/12 is one half and 7/12 is one twelfth more. Partial: saying more with no use of 6/12 is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparing to the benchmark 1/2)","1/4, 1/3, 3/8. In twenty-fourths they are 6/24, 8/24, and 9/24. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","No. The two fractions name parts of different wholes, because her row is twice as long as his. A comparison of fractions is only valid when both name parts of the same whole. To answer it you would have to work out the actual lengths. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparisons are valid only for the same whole)"]}]},{id:8,title:"A Herculean Job",short:"Word Work",parts:[{std:["RL.4.4"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story again. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases as used in a story, including an allusion to a character from mythology",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Mr. Ferreira calls the mulch “a Herculean job.” Hercules was a hero in Greek myths who was famous for his strength and for finishing twelve enormous tasks. What does Herculean mean here, and what in the story shows it?","Dev says “his whole May was a blank page.” What does that mean? Is there really a page?","Rosalind says “One for one.” What does she mean by it?","The story says Rosalind “had been counting down to it for a month.” What does counting down tell you about how she felt?","Mr. Ferreira “read the list the way he reads everything, slowly, from the top.” What does that phrase tell you about him?"],key:["Herculean means enormous, needing great strength or effort. The name comes from Hercules, so calling the mulch Herculean says it was a job that took the strength of a hero. The story shows it: “Nine wheelbarrows, by myself, while he was at the dentist.” (RL.4.4, a word that alludes to a character from mythology)","No real page. It is a way of saying his May was empty, with nothing written on it and nothing planned, so one more Saturday cost him nothing. (RL.4.4, figurative language)","An even trade: she will take one of his Saturdays in exchange for the one of hers he is taking, so neither of them owes the other. The story shows it in the next line, when she writes her name on the twelfth. (RL.4.4, meaning of a phrase as used in the text)","That she had been looking forward to it for a long time and knew exactly how many days were left, which is why giving up that morning mattered to her. (RL.4.4, meaning of a phrase in context)","That he is careful and unhurried, and that he does the same thing every time rather than skimming. It also explains why he does not answer her right away. (RL.4.4, meaning of a phrase in context)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Tenths and Hundredths",short:"Decimals",parts:[{title:"Write It as a Decimal",std:["4.NF.C.6"],dur:110,dir:"Use the grid and the number line. Answer out loud, or write the answers on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"writing fractions with denominator 10 or 100 in decimal notation",html:()=>lead("The crew measures stakes and rows with a meter stick. The big square below is one whole meter cut into 100 equal pieces."),fig:()=>stackFig(group(F.gridRect(10,10,43,{cell:26}),"A whole cut into 100 equal parts, 43 shaded")+group(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.1,width:620}),"One meter in tenths")),qs:["Write 7/10 as a decimal.","Look at the grid. Write the shaded part as a fraction with 100 on the bottom, then as a decimal.","Write 0.06 as a fraction.","Rewrite 3/10 as a fraction in hundredths, then write it as a decimal.","A stake is 62/100 of a meter long. Write that as a decimal. Between which two tenths does it sit on the number line?","A row is 9/10 of a meter. Say it in hundredths and write it as a decimal."],key:["0.7. Or: 7/10 is 70/100, which is 0.70. (4.NF.C.6)","43/100 and 0.43. The grid shows 43 of the 100 equal parts shaded. Partial: one of the fraction and the decimal given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","6/100. Partial: 6/10 puts the 6 in the tenths place and does not count. (4.NF.C.6)","30/100, which is 0.30. That is the same number as 0.3. Partial: 30/100 right with no decimal given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","0.62. It sits between 0.6 and 0.7 on the number line, just past the 0.6 tick, because 2 more hundredths is only a fifth of the way to 0.7. Partial: 0.62 right with no pair of tenths named is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","90/100, written 0.90, which is the same number as 0.9. Partial: 9/100 puts the 9 in the hundredths place and does not count. (4.NF.C.6)"]},{title:"Which Is More?",std:["4.NF.C.7"],dur:110,dir:"Use the two grids. Answer out loud and say how you know.",desc:"comparing two decimals to hundredths by reasoning about their size",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:30px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.gridRect(10,10,60,{cell:22}),"Grid P")}${group(F.gridRect(10,10,56,{cell:22}),"Grid Q")}</div>`,html:()=>lead("Grid P and Grid Q are the same size. Each grid is one whole cut into 100 equal parts."),qs:["Grid P shows 0.6 and Grid Q shows 0.56. Which is greater? Use the grids to explain.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.3 ____ 0.30","Put these in order from least to greatest: 0.09, 0.9, 0.19","One stake is 0.45 meters long and another is 0.54 meters long. Which is longer?","Why is 0.7 greater than 0.68, even though 68 is a bigger number than 7?","Compare 0.8 of a meter and 0.08 of a meter. How many times longer is the first one?"],key:["0.6 is greater. 0.6 is 6 tenths, which is 60 of the 100 small squares, and 0.56 is 56 of them. Grid P has 4 more squares shaded. Partial: naming 0.6 with no count from the grids is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","0.3 = 0.30. Three tenths is the same as thirty hundredths. Partial: making one of them the greater counts digits instead of places and does not count. (4.NF.C.7)","0.09, 0.19, 0.9. In hundredths they are 9, 19, and 90. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","The 0.54 stake. Compare the tenths first: 5 tenths beats 4 tenths. Partial: naming the stake with no reason from the tenths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","Because the digits have to be lined up by place, not read as whole numbers. 0.7 is 70 hundredths and 0.68 is 68 hundredths, so 0.7 is 2 hundredths more. Partial: naming the places with no count in hundredths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","0.8 is greater, and it is ten times longer. 0.8 is 80 hundredths and 0.08 is 8 hundredths. Partial: naming 0.8 with no account of how many times longer it is is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Hose, Barrel, Bag",short:"Units",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.1"],dur:150,dir:"Solve each one on your paper and tell your teacher the answer with its unit. For the last one, make the table on your paper.",desc:"knowing relative sizes of measurement units and converting from a larger unit to a smaller one",html:()=>lead("Everything in the shed is labeled in the bigger unit. The jobs need the smaller one."),fig:()=>F.row([F.table(["Bigger unit","equals","Smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"]]),"What the shed label card says"]),qs:["The hose is 24 meters long. How many centimeters is that?","A watering can holds 8 liters. How many milliliters is that?","A bag of bean seed weighs 3 kilograms. How many grams is that?","The work morning runs 2 hours. How many minutes is that?","A stake board is 5 feet long. How many inches is that?","Make a two-column table on your paper with the headings Kilograms and Grams, and fill in the rows for 1, 2, 5, and 9 kilograms."],key:["2,400 centimeters. 1 meter is 100 centimeters, so 24 × 100 = 2,400. Partial: 240 uses tens instead of hundreds and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","8,000 milliliters. 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 8 × 1,000 = 8,000. Partial: 800 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","3,000 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 3 × 1,000 = 3,000. Partial: 300 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","120 minutes. 1 hour is 60 minutes, so 2 × 60 = 120. Partial: 120 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","60 inches. 1 foot is 12 inches, so 5 × 12 = 60. Partial: 60 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","Kilograms 1, 2, 5, 9 against Grams 1,000, 2,000, 5,000, 9,000. The table has to have both headings and all four pairs. Going from the bigger unit to the smaller one, the number gets bigger, because each unit is smaller. Partial: three of the four pairs right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, recording measurement equivalents in a two-column table)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Fix the Handbook",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.4.1"],dur:85,dir:"Each line from the garden handbook has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (relative pronouns, modal auxiliaries, adjective order, confused words, fragments)",qs:["Dev is the one which took the first Saturday.","We should of filled the barrels first.","The shed holds two green big barrels.","The cocoa was to hot to drink.","Because the gate was already open."],key:["Dev is the one who took the first Saturday. who is the relative pronoun for a person. (L.4.1a, relative pronouns)","We should have filled the barrels first. should of is not a correct form, so the modal auxiliary needs have. (L.4.1c, modal auxiliaries)","The shed holds two big green barrels. Number, then size, then color, then the noun. (L.4.1d, ordering adjectives)","The cocoa was too hot to drink. too means more than enough. (L.4.1g, frequently confused words)","A fragment. Any complete sentence that uses the clause, such as: We went in because the gate was already open. (L.4.1f, correcting a fragment)"]},{title:"Say It Right",std:["L.4.1"],dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (progressive tense, relative adverbs, prepositional phrases, run-on sentences)",qs:["Change this sentence so it tells what was happening at nine o'clock yesterday: Dev waits under the eave.","Which word fits? The shed ____ the rota hangs is behind bed one. Is it where or which?","Make one sentence out of these two, using a phrase that tells where: The barrels are full. The barrels are at each end of the beds.","Fix this run-on sentence: Rosalind opened the gate it was raining hard."],key:["Dev was waiting under the eave. (L.4.1b, past progressive verb tense)","where. where is the relative adverb for a place. (L.4.1a, relative adverbs)","The barrels at each end of the beds are full. Accept any correct version that keeps the prepositional phrase, such as: At each end of the beds, the barrels are full. (L.4.1e, prepositional phrases)","Any correct fix, such as: Rosalind opened the gate, but it was raining hard. Or two sentences: Rosalind opened the gate. It was raining hard. (L.4.1f, correcting a run-on)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Two Sources",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Read both sources. Take your notes on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"taking notes from two sources, sorting them into categories, and listing the sources",html:()=>`${twoSources()}<p class="big">Make two boxes on your paper and label them like the boxes below. Write two short notes in each box, and mark each note with the source it came from, Source 1 or Source 2. Under the boxes, write a list of your two sources.</p>${noteBoxes("What the rota is for","What a person on the rota has to do")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">A note is a few words in your own words, not a sentence copied out.</p>`,key:["What the rota is for box: two notes, such as says whose turn it is (Source 1), closes the gaps so no Saturday is missed (Source 1), spreads the work so seven share it instead of three (Source 1), the sheet is the only record the garden keeps (Source 2). (W.4.8, categorizes information)","What a person has to do box: two notes, such as be there at eight and open the gate (Source 1 or 2), fill both barrels first (Source 2), put the job list on the gate (Source 2), lock up at the end (Source 2), find a swap and write it in pencil (Source 1 or 2). (W.4.8, categorizes information)","Each note is brief and in the student's own words, and each note is marked with the source it came from (W.4.8, takes notes and paraphrases)","A list of both sources at the bottom, naming at least the title and the author or the publisher: “What a Rota Does,” by Imogen Park, Westmill Register, March 2026; and Westmill Community Garden Handbook, 2026 edition, page 4. (W.4.8, provides a list of sources)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Shed Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Keep the rota or go back?"},std:["W.4.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">The garden has to decide what to do next season. Should it keep the rota, or go back to open Saturdays where anyone comes when they can? Write your opinion. Use the fact box. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: the two ways",["<strong>With the rota.</strong> 28 Saturdays last season. 9 of them were swapped. Not one Saturday went unopened. Seven people share the season, four Saturdays each.","<strong>Open Saturdays, the old way.</strong> Some Saturdays twenty people came and got in each other's way. Some Saturdays nobody came, and the beds went four days without water. Three people opened the gate on most Saturdays.","Three people have told the garden they stopped coming on Saturdays after the rota started, because they did not want to promise a date months ahead.","Either way, the beds still need water every Saturday in the summer."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic and state your opinion clearly","Give reasons that are backed by facts from the fact box","Group your reasons so related ideas sit together","Use linking words: because, for instance, in order to, in addition","End with a conclusion that fits your opinion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion about keeping the rota or going back (W.4.1a)","Reasons are grouped so that related ideas sit together (W.4.1a)","Supports the opinion with facts and details from the fact box, such as 9 swaps out of 28 with no Saturday unopened, or the three people who stopped coming (W.4.1b)","Links opinion and reasons with words and phrases such as because, for instance, in order to, in addition (W.4.1c)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the opinion (W.4.1d)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach someone how a rota works"},std:["W.4.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">A new family has joined the garden and nobody has explained the rota to them. Write to teach them what a rota is, how the garden's rota works, and what to do if their Saturday does not work out. Use facts from the article. Write 7 to 9 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Start by naming your topic, and group related information together","Give facts, definitions, and details from the article","Use linking words: also, another, for example, because","Use words that belong to the topic, such as rota, swap, season","End with a sentence that wraps it up"])}${lookBack("the article",rotaHtml(true))}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and groups related information together (W.4.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, definitions, and details from the article, such as what a rota is, seven names at a time, 28 Saturdays in the season, open the gate at eight (W.4.2b)","Explains the swap: find someone, write it on the sheet in pencil (W.4.2b)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as also, another, for example, because (W.4.2c)","Uses words that belong to the topic and ends with a concluding statement (W.4.2d and W.4.2e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"You showed up and almost nobody did"},std:["W.4.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:210,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You promised to be somewhere on a Saturday morning. The weather is terrible, and when you get there only one other person has come. Write the story of that morning. Write 7 to 9 sentences.</p>${pills("First","A few minutes later","After that","By the end")}${checklist(["Set up the situation: who you are, where you are, and who else is there","Tell the events in an order that makes sense","Use dialogue and description to show what happened","Use words that show time moving: at first, a few minutes later, by the time, finally","End by telling how the morning turned out and what you thought about it"])}`,key:["Orients the reader by setting up the situation and introducing the narrator and the other character (W.4.3a)","Events unfold naturally in an order that makes sense (W.4.3a)","Uses dialogue and description to show what the people did, said, or felt (W.4.3b)","Uses words and phrases that signal the order of events, such as at first, a few minutes later, by the time, finally (W.4.3c)","Uses concrete words and sensory details, and ends with a conclusion that follows from the events (W.4.3d and W.4.3e)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Both Sources at Once",short:"Both Sources",parts:[{std:["RI.4.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at both sources. Answer out loud, and say which source each part comes from.",desc:"pulling information from two texts on the same topic to speak about it knowledgeably",html:()=>twoSources(),qs:["Using both sources, tell me everything a person on the rota has to do on their Saturday.","Both sources talk about pencil. Put the two together: what is the pencil rule, and why does it matter?","One source tells you why the garden started using a rota. Which one, and what does it say?"],key:["Open the gate at eight, fill both barrels first, put the job list on the gate, and lock up when the last person leaves, all from Source 2, plus the one job named in Source 1, be there at eight and open the gate. Count it correct when the answer uses both sources","Source 2 says write a swap on the sheet in pencil and never cross a name out in pen, because the sheet is the only record the garden keeps. Source 1 says the pencil matters because 9 swaps out of 28 Saturdays got written and not one Saturday went unopened. The integrated answer links the rule to the result","Source 1. For two summers anyone came who felt like it, so some Saturdays twenty people got in each other's way and some Saturdays nobody came and the beds went four days without water"]}]},{id:15,title:"The Seed Swap",short:"Paraphrase",parts:[{title:"Say It Back",std:["SL.4.2"],n:3,dur:60,read:SEED_SWAP,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then say it back in your own words.",desc:"paraphrasing a text read aloud",html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read about a seed swap. Listen, then put it in your own words."),key:["Ask: What happens at the seed swap, and who may take seed? Paraphrase: people bring seed they saved and lay it out on tables, nothing is sold, and anyone may take seed even if they brought none. Count it correct only when the words are the student's own","Ask: Put the rule the organizers repeat into your own words. Paraphrase: seed does no good sitting unused in a drawer, so it should be passed on and planted","Ask: Give me the main idea in one or two sentences of your own. Paraphrase: the garden holds a free yearly swap so saved seed gets used, and it is popular, with 64 people last February and the tables cleared by half past ten","A sentence copied back word for word is not a paraphrase. If that happens, say: Now tell me the same thing without using those words."]},{title:"Report It",std:["SL.4.4"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:50,dir:"Talk for about one minute. Speak clearly and at a pace your teacher can follow.",desc:"reporting on a topic in an organized way with facts and descriptive details",html:()=>`<p class="big">Tell me about something you and other people do together, such as a team, a class job, a club, or a chore everyone at home shares. How does it get organized. What is your part in it?</p>`,key:["Organized: there is a beginning, a middle and an end, not a pile of facts in any order","Includes appropriate facts, such as how many people, how often, and who decides what","Includes relevant descriptive details, and speaks clearly at a pace you can follow. If the answer is two sentences, ask once: What is your part in it?"]}]},{id:16,title:"Times as Many",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the last answer on your paper.",desc:"interpreting and writing multiplication as a comparison",qs:["The crew moved 9 wheelbarrows of mulch and 3 wheelbarrows of compost. The number of mulch loads is how many times the number of compost loads?","What does the equation 35 = 5 x 7 say as a comparison?","Write an equation for this sentence: 48 is 6 times as many as 8."],key:["3 times as many. 9 = 3 x 3. Partial: 6 more is a difference and not a multiplicative comparison, so it does not count.","35 is 5 times as many as 7. Saying 35 is 7 times as many as 5 is also correct. Partial: 35 is 5 more than 7 reads it as an addition and does not count.","48 = 6 x 8. Writing 6 x 8 = 48 counts too. Partial: 48 = 6 + 8 reads it as an addition and does not count."]}]},{id:17,title:"Round the Season",short:"Rounding",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"rounding multi-digit whole numbers to a named place",qs:["The garden weighed 1,480 pounds of food last season. Round that to the nearest hundred. Now round it to the nearest thousand.","Round 4,728 to the nearest ten.","Round 26,350 to the nearest thousand. How did you decide?"],key:["1,500 to the nearest hundred, because 80 is past halfway to the next hundred. 1,000 to the nearest thousand, because 480 is less than halfway to 2,000. Partial: one of the two roundings right is approaching.","4,730. Partial: 4,720 rounds down when the ones digit is 8 and does not count.","26,000. The digit in the hundreds place is 3, which is less than 5, so the thousands digit stays the same. Partial: 27,000 rounds up when the hundreds digit is 3 and does not count."]}]},{id:18,title:"Bed Size",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer with its unit.",desc:"using the area and perimeter formulas for rectangles in real problems",qs:["A raised bed is 12 feet long and 4 feet wide. What is its area? What is its perimeter?","The crew wants a low fence all the way around a bed that is 15 feet by 3 feet. How many feet of fencing do they need?","A bed has an area of 60 square feet. One side is 5 feet long. How long is the other side?"],key:["Area 48 square feet (12 x 4). Perimeter 32 feet (12 + 4 + 12 + 4, or 2 x 12 plus 2 x 4). Both units have to be right. Partial: one of the area and the perimeter right is approaching.","36 feet (2 x 15 plus 2 x 3). This is perimeter, not area. Partial: 45 square feet gives the area instead of the perimeter and does not count.","12 feet (60 divided by 5). Look for the student working backward from the area formula. Partial: 12 with no unit named is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Plot Shapes",short:"Shapes",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the four plots. Answer out loud.",desc:"classifying figures by parallel sides, perpendicular sides, and right angles, and spotting a right triangle",fig:()=>plotShapes(),qs:["Which plots have at least one pair of parallel sides?","Which plot is a right triangle? How do you know?","Plot 4 has no parallel sides. Does it have a right angle? What about plot 2?"],key:["Plot 1 and plot 2. The rectangle has two pairs of parallel sides, and so does the slanted one. Partial: one of the two plots named is approaching.","Plot 3. Two of its sides meet in a square corner, which is a right angle, and the other two angles are smaller than a right angle. Partial: naming Plot 3 with no reason is approaching.","No, plot 4 has three angles and none of them is square. Plot 2 has no right angle either: its corners lean, two of them wider than square and two of them narrower. Partial: one of the two plots answered is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Butterfly and Rabbit",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two animals. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">Volunteers planting the park meadow find a butterfly on a flower and a rabbit under the hedge.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("butterfly",104)}${F.icon("rabbit",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The butterfly has a long coiled mouthpart like a straw, wide wings, and two antennae. The rabbit has large ears, strong back legs, and a long gut inside its body.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the butterfly's straw shaped mouthpart and the job of its wings.","Build an argument that the rabbit's long gut matters for an animal that eats grass and leaves.","The rabbit's ears are large and can turn. What does that structure do for it, and is it inside or outside?"],key:["The coiled mouthpart reaches nectar deep inside a flower, which is how the butterfly feeds. The wings carry it between flowers and away from anything that comes near, and they also let it reach new places to lay eggs. (4-LS1-1)","Grass and leaves are tough and hard to get energy out of, so food has to stay inside longer. A long gut gives more time and more room to break the plant material down, so the rabbit can live on food other animals cannot use. Look for the claim plus the reasoning. (4-LS1-1)","Large turning ears collect faint sounds from many directions and tell the rabbit where a sound came from. They are an outside structure. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">The rabbit is feeding at the edge of the new meadow. A volunteer's wheelbarrow squeaks on the path twenty meters away. The rabbit freezes, turns one ear toward the path, waits, then goes back to feeding.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rabbit",104)}${F.icon("purpleFlower",104)}</div>`,qs:["Which sense took in the information, and which body part took it in?","Freezing and then going back to feeding are two different responses. What did the brain do between them?","Build a three step model of the path and say where the decision happened."],key:["Hearing, taken in by the ears. Turning an ear toward the path is a response that gathers better information. (4-LS1-2)","It processed the sound, worked out where it came from and whether it mattered, and then chose a different response when nothing more happened. Both freezing and feeding again were choices made in the brain. (4-LS1-2)","The ears take in the sound, the brain processes it and chooses a response, the body carries the response out. The decision happens in the brain, which is the heart of this standard. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:21,title:"The Park Creek Bank",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the bank. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">The creek that runs through the park has cut a bank four layers deep. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What the layer is</th><th>What is found in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>dark soil</td><td>roots, grass seeds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>peat, dark and spongy</td><td>reed stems and water beetle shells</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>coarse sand and gravel</td><td>broken river clam shells</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>stiff gray clay</td><td>oyster shells</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer has been there the longest, and how does the chart show it?","Oysters live in salty bays. What does the bottom layer tell you about this park long ago?","Use all four layers in order to explain how this place changed."],key:["The stiff gray clay at the bottom, because layers build upward and the ones above landed later. (4-ESS1-1)","A salty bay reached this spot. Oysters need salt or brackish water, so the sea must have come further inland than it does now. (4-ESS1-1)","A salty bay left clay with oysters, then a river laid down coarse sand and gravel with freshwater clams, then a still marsh built up peat with reeds and water beetles, then it dried enough for soil and grass. Any account reading the chart upward and naming the changes counts. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion under different conditions",html:()=>`<p class="big">The volunteers are losing the gravel path into the creek every time it rains. They tested four short sections of path and ran the same four liters of water down each, then weighed what came off the end.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Section</th><th>What was different</th><th>Material washed off</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>loose gravel, steep section</td><td>240 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>loose gravel, gentle section</td><td>88 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>gravel packed down hard, steep section</td><td>95 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>steep section with low plants along both edges</td><td>36 grams</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which pair of sections shows what the slope does? Give the numbers and what they show.","Which pair shows what packing the gravel does, and what is the answer?","Section 4 lost the least. Explain what the plants along the edges were doing."],key:["Sections 1 and 2, both loose gravel: 240 grams on the steep section and 88 grams on the gentle one. Only the slope changed, so a steeper path erodes faster. (4-ESS2-1)","Sections 1 and 3, both steep: 240 grams loose and 95 grams packed. Packing the gravel down cut the loss by more than half, because tightly packed pieces are harder for water to lift. (4-ESS2-1)","Roots held the soil and gravel at the edges, and the leaves and stems slowed the water and spread it out instead of letting it run in a fast channel down the middle. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The River on the Map",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart from the river map. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">A map follows one river from the hills to the bay. For each stretch it gives how much the land falls and how wide the flat ground beside the river is.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",80)}${F.icon("bridge",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Stretch</th><th>Fall in height over 10 km</th><th>Width of flat ground beside the river</th><th>Shape of the river on the map</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 in the hills</td><td>180 m</td><td>almost none</td><td>straight, in a narrow canyon</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 foothills</td><td>60 m</td><td>300 m</td><td>slight bends</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3 valley</td><td>8 m</td><td>3 km</td><td>wide looping bends</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 near the bay</td><td>1 m</td><td>9 km</td><td>very wide loops, side channels, marsh</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern that links the fall in height to the shape of the river.","Describe what happens to the width of the flat ground from stretch 1 to stretch 4.","A town is to be built on stretch 4. Use the map pattern to say what the planners should know about that ground."],key:["Where the land falls steeply the river runs straight in a narrow canyon. As the fall gets gentler the river bends more, until near the bay it loops widely and splits into side channels. (4-ESS2-2)","It grows steadily, from almost none in the canyon to 300 meters, then 3 kilometers, then 9 kilometers of flat ground near the bay. (4-ESS2-2)","Wide flat ground beside a slow looping river is land the river itself built and still floods. Building there means building on a flood plain, which the map pattern shows plainly. Using the pattern to reason about a new place is the point. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">Heavy rain floods the low corner of the park and the path there is under water for a week at a time. The neighborhood group can fund one plan.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost</th><th>Side effect</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: dig a rain garden, a shallow planted basin</td><td>holds the water and lets it soak in slowly instead of running across the path</td><td>low, one volunteer weekend</td><td>that corner becomes a wet meadow, not lawn</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: raise the path on a low wooden walkway</td><td>keeps the path usable while the water sits under it</td><td>high, needs a builder</td><td>the water still stands, the path just rises above it</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third plan of your own for the flooded corner.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you fund. What in the chart supports it?","Put your own plan, plan A and plan B in order from cheapest to most expensive. Then say whether the cheapest of the three is good enough for this corner."],key:["Any workable idea: lay a gravel bed under the path so water drains through, pipe the water to the creek, regrade the corner so water runs off, move the path to higher ground, plant trees that drink a lot of water. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A is cheap, volunteers can do it, and it reduces the flooding itself, though the corner changes from lawn to wet meadow. Plan B keeps the path open but costs much more and does nothing about the standing water. The reason from the chart is what is rated. (4-ESS3-2)","Of the two on the chart, A is the cheaper at one volunteer weekend and B the dearer at a builder's price, and the student's own plan has to be placed by a cost it can argue for rather than guessed at. Then a judgment with a reason attached: cheapest is good enough only when it reduces the flooding, and the chart says A does while B leaves the water standing, so here the cheap plan is also the one that works. Or: a student whose own plan is cheaper still and says so counts, as long as it is placed and judged on the same measure. Partial: an order given with nothing said about whether the cheapest is good enough is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Lights for the Community Hall",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">The hall and the park paths can be lit and heated in four ways. The group made a chart before voting.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Option</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>Runs out?</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>mains electricity from a gas power station</td><td>natural gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>gases into the air at the station</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mains electricity from a hydro dam</td><td>moving water from rain and snowmelt</td><td>no</td><td>the dam floods a valley and blocks fish</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a propane heater in the hall</td><td>gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>gases into the air, tanks trucked in</td></tr>
     <tr><td>solar path lights with small batteries</td><td>sunlight</td><td>no</td><td>batteries must be made and replaced</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Pick two options and trace each one back to the natural resource it starts from.","Two of these four are renewable, but both still change the environment. Name the change for each.","The group chooses solar path lights instead of mains lights. Name one effect that gets smaller and one new effect that appears."],key:["Any two read correctly: gas station electricity from natural gas underground, hydro electricity from moving water fed by rain and snow, the propane heater from gas underground, the path lights from sunlight. (4-ESS3-1)","The hydro dam floods a valley and blocks fish moving up and down the river. The solar lights need small batteries that have to be manufactured and replaced, and worn batteries must be handled properly. (4-ESS3-1)","Fewer gases from the power station for that part of the school's use. New effects: the batteries have to be made, replaced and recycled, and the lights give less light at the end of a cloudy winter day. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"The Donation Wagon",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">Volunteers roll the same loaded wagon down the hall ramp and let it run into a padded stop block. They measured the wagon's speed and how far the block was pushed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Try</th><th>Speed at the bottom</th><th>Block pushed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>1 meter each second</td><td>2 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>2 meters each second</td><td>8 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>3 meters each second</td><td>19 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to the push distance as the wagon's speed goes up?","Which try carried the most energy? Give the measurement that shows it.","The volunteers decide to walk the wagon down slowly instead of letting it roll. Explain, using the chart, why that is safer for the load."],key:["It grows, and faster each time: 2 cm, 8 cm, 19 cm. (4-PS3-1)","Try 3, at 3 meters each second, because it pushed the block 19 cm, the farthest of the three. (4-PS3-1)","A slower wagon has much less energy, so far less energy goes into whatever it strikes at the bottom. The chart shows that going three times as fast pushed the block about ten times as far, so even a small rise in speed means a much harder knock for the load. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two carts stand in the hall. One is loaded with boxes of blankets and is rolling slowly. The other is empty and still, right in its path.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("box",96)}${F.icon("pack",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to the empty cart and to the loaded one.","A folded blanket is taped to the front of the loaded cart. Predict what that changes about the collision, and say why."],key:["Any testable question: how far does the empty cart roll, is the bump louder at a higher speed, what if both carts are loaded, does a carpeted floor change the result. (4-PS3-3)","The empty cart is pushed forward and rolls away. The loaded cart slows down. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","The blanket spreads the push out over a longer moment and soaks up some of the energy, so the knock is softer, there is less sound, and less of the energy goes into denting the carts. Energy going into squashing the blanket rather than into a sharp bang is the idea. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Energy in the Hall",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy traveled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed at the community hall during a volunteer afternoon.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>The soup pot has been on the stove a while, and the metal handle is now too hot to hold.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>Somebody claps for quiet and everyone in the hall turns around.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The window blind is raised and the whole back wall brightens.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>A lead runs from the socket to the kettle, and the water begins to heat.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By heat, from the stove into the pot and along the metal to the handle. (4-PS3-2)","By sound, through the air from the hands to every ear in the hall. (4-PS3-2)","By light, from outside through the window to the back wall. (4-PS3-2)","By an electric current, through the lead from the socket to the kettle element, where it becomes heat. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The group wants a radio at the park clean up. There is no power and no spare batteries. Design a device that turns one kind of energy into electrical energy to run the radio. You may use a hand crank, a bicycle wheel, or a small generator. You may also use wire, a propeller, or a panel that takes in sunlight.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("radio",96)}${F.icon("bike",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the energy going in and the electrical energy coming out.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it and the radio cuts out whenever the person stops pedalling. Name one change and say why it should help."],key:["Any labeled drawing with a path: a bicycle wheel turning a small generator wired to the radio; a crank and generator; a propeller in the wind; a sunlight panel wired in. Labels and a path make it a design. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: motion from pedalling or cranking, or light from the sun. Coming out: electrical energy, plus some heat and sound. Both forms named. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: add a small battery or capacitor that stores energy and keeps the radio going; add a heavy flywheel so the generator keeps spinning between pushes; gear it so a slow pedal still spins the generator fast. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Water, Reflectors and a Buddy Code",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A cork floats in a long tray of water. The group tapped one end of the tray gently, then firmly, and drew the waves that crossed it.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two waves drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first wave">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">gentle tap</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q30 -24 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="50" y1="70" x2="50" y2="58" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="62" y="64" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">A</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="140" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="80" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">B</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">firm tap</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q30 -76 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the amplitude, and which marks the wavelength?","Which measurement changed between the two taps? Say whether it grew or shrank.","The cork barely moves with the gentle tap and bobs high with the firm one. Explain why, using the drawings."],key:["A marks the amplitude, from the flat line to the crest. B marks the wavelength, from one crest to the next. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude grew. The crests are much taller after the firm tap. The wavelength stayed the same, which is worth noting if the student does not see it. (4-PS4-1)","The firm tap put more energy into the water, which shows as a bigger amplitude. A wave with more energy moves the cork farther up and down. The cork still bobs in place rather than traveling along the tray. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">The group puts reflective strips on the park gate posts. Then walkers can see them at night. A flashlight is shone down the path. It makes the strips glow brightly. The plain wooden posts stay dim.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("key",96)}${F.icon("lamp",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the path of the light when a walker sees a reflective strip. Name every place it goes.","Why does the strip look so much brighter than the plain wood beside it?","With every light off, the strips cannot be seen at all. What does that prove about them?"],key:["Light leaves the flashlight, travels to the strip, reflects off the strip, and travels into the walker's eye. All three legs are needed. (4-PS4-2)","The strip sends most of the light that hits it back the way it came, so far more light from the flashlight reaches the walker's eye. The plain wood scatters and absorbs most of the light, so little arrives at the eye. (4-PS4-2)","They do not make their own light. They only reflect light that comes from somewhere else, which is why they need a flashlight or a headlight to work. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">Buddy pairs working across the park need three messages: all finished here, bring more bags, and come back to the hall.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Whistle blasts</td><td>one short, two short, or one long blast</td><td>heard across the whole park; hard to tell who sent it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Colored bandana on a stick</td><td>a green cloth, a yellow cloth, or a cloth waved side to side</td><td>daylight and a clear view; shows exactly which group is signalling</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","Name one job each system does better than the other.","Design a third system of your own for the same three messages."],key:["In the whistle system the pattern is the number of blasts and whether a blast is short or long. In the cloth system it is the color shown and whether it is held still or waved. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The whistle reaches the whole park and works when nobody is looking that way. The cloth shows exactly which group is signalling and where they are, which a whistle cannot do. (4-PS4-3)","Any pattern with three agreed signals: one, two or three flashes of a flashlight, a card with one, two or three marks held up, three positions of an arm, three agreed words on a radio, a bag tied at one of three heights on the fence. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"A Cart for the Volunteers",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Volunteers carry tools, seed trays and full water jugs across the park by hand. The jugs slop and the trays tip. The group has one Saturday, an old pushchair frame, scrap plywood, rope, and donated materials only.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wateringCan",88)}${F.icon("box",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the group the cart works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: volunteers need to move tools, trays and full jugs across the park in one trip without spilling or tipping. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: carries two full jugs with no spill, holds four seed trays level, one person can push it over grass, it fits through the park gate. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One Saturday, only the pushchair frame, plywood and rope that were donated, nothing bought. Any two. Giving a criterion here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs for the cart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Build time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>a flat plywood deck on the pushchair frame, with a rope rail around the edge</td><td>frame, plywood, rope</td><td>half a day</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>a plywood box with slots that hold each jug upright and a shelf above for the trays</td><td>frame, plywood</td><td>most of the day</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>a small powered garden trolley</td><td>a trolley to buy</td><td>order and wait</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design breaks a constraint? Name the constraint.","Compare designs 1 and 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to spill less, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own that fits the materials and the time."],key:["Design 3. It has to be bought and only donated materials may be used, and the order would not arrive on the one Saturday. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Design 2, because slots hold each jug upright so it cannot lean and slop, and the shelf keeps the trays level. The flat deck with a rope rail stops things falling off but lets them slide and tilt. Look for reasoning from the criteria. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials given: cut round holes in the plywood deck so the jugs drop in and sit low, add a low rim all round, lash the jugs to the frame with rope, make the deck wider and lower so the load is stable, fit a second small deck below for the heavy tools. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The group wants to find out which cart spills less on the way across the park. Both carts are built.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",96)}${F.icon("board",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure?","Name two things you must keep the same for both carts so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your cart is most likely to give way first."],key:["Any measured outcome: weigh the jugs before and after the trip and find the water lost, count the trays that tipped, measure how far it goes before the first spill. A number, not an impression. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same route across the park, the same load with the same amount of water, the same person pushing, the same speed, the same day and the same ground conditions. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: the plywood deck cracks under a heavy jug, the rope rail works loose, a wheel of the old frame collapses, the joint where the deck meets the frame splits, the shelf sags when wet. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"California Waters",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four California bodies of water. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the physical and human geographic features that define places and regions in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four bodies of water in California, and what sits around each one.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("drop",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Water</th><th>Region</th><th>Fresh or salty</th><th>What people have built or done there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>San Francisco Bay</td><td>Coast</td><td>salty, open to the ocean</td><td>ports, bridges, cities, a dredged shipping channel inland</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Lake Tahoe</td><td>Sierra Nevada, 1,897 m up</td><td>fresh, very deep and clear</td><td>a highway round it, towns, water rules to protect the clarity</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mono Lake</td><td>east of the Sierra, in a dry basin</td><td>salty, with no outlet to the sea</td><td>streams once diverted to Los Angeles, now partly returned by law</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Salton Sea</td><td>southern desert, 71 m below sea level</td><td>salty and getting saltier</td><td>farm drainage flows into it, and it has no outlet</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Two of these are salty but are not part of the ocean. Name them and explain how a lake can be salty.","Lake Tahoe sits at 1,897 meters and the Salton Sea at 71 meters below sea level. Name the region each belongs to.","Mono Lake's streams were once diverted to a city far away. What does that tell you about how people in California use water?","Name one physical feature and one human feature from the chart. Then say what makes each one that kind."],key:["Mono Lake and the Salton Sea. Water flows in carrying dissolved minerals but has no outlet to the sea, so it can only leave by evaporating. The minerals stay behind and build up, which makes the water salty. Partial: both lakes named with no account of how a lake turns salty is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Lake Tahoe is in the Sierra Nevada mountains. The Salton Sea is in the southern desert. Partial: one of the two regions named is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Water is moved long distances from where it falls to where people want it, even across a mountain range. It also shows that those choices can be changed, since some of the flow was returned by law. Partial: describing the diversion with nothing said about what it shows is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical: the bay, the lakes, the elevations, the salt. Human: ports, bridges, highways, the dredged channel, the diversions and the water rules. Physical comes from nature, human is built or decided by people. Partial: one of each named with no difference stated is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Working Together in Early California",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:175,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the social and economic life and the interactions among the peoples of early California",html:()=>`<p class="big">How work was shared in three periods of early California.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>Who did the work</th><th>How the work was shared or directed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>First peoples, for thousands of years</td><td>everyone in a village had a part: gathering, fishing, hunting, basket making, tool making</td><td>families and villages shared the harvest; nations traded with one another for what their own area lacked</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spanish mission period, 1769 onward</td><td>Native Californians brought to the missions did the farming, herding and workshop work</td><td>the work was directed by the Spanish Franciscan missionaries and the soldiers, not chosen by the workers; the Franciscans had been sent to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish trades, and the work was part of that purpose</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mexican rancho period, 1820s onward</td><td>Native Californian vaqueros handled the cattle; rancho families held the land grants</td><td>vaqueros were skilled workers on land held by someone else, often paid in goods rather than money</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Among the first peoples, some work was set aside for a nation's own religious ceremonies. Chumash ceremonies were led by trained specialists who did that work and little else. Miwok nations built a large round earth covered ceremonial house used only for their dances. Each nation had its own religious beliefs and its own ceremonies rather than one shared religion.</p>`,qs:["Name one big difference between how work was shared among the first peoples and how it was directed at the missions. According to the chart, what had the Franciscan missionaries been sent to California to do?","Vaqueros were highly skilled. Where would those skills have come from? And according to the note, name one kind of work a nation set aside for its own ceremonies.","Trade between nations let each one get what its own area lacked. Give an example of what a coastal nation and an inland nation might each have wanted."],key:["Among the first peoples people had parts within their own village and families shared the harvest, and nations chose what to trade. At the missions the work was directed by others and was not the workers' own choice. The Franciscans had been sent to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish trades. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the work comparison with nothing on the Franciscans sits there. (HSS-4.2)","From years of working with horses and cattle on the open range, and from knowledge of the land, water and seasons that Native Californians already had from living there. Skill built up through the work and was passed on. On the ceremonies: the trained specialists who led Chumash ceremonies, or the building of the Miwok round ceremonial house that was used for nothing but the dances. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the source of the skills with nothing on the ceremonies sits there. (HSS-4.2)","A coastal nation had shellfish, fish, shell beads and salt and might want obsidian for blades, pine nuts or acorns from inland. An inland nation had obsidian, pine nuts and deer hides and might want shell beads and dried fish. Any sensible pair based on what each place provides. Partial: one side of the pair given is approaching. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the social and political life of California from the Bear Flag Republic through the Gold Rush to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">What mining camps built for themselves between 1848 and 1855.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846</td><td>The Bear Flag Republic was declared at Sonoma and lasted weeks, until the war with Mexico reached California.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848 and 1849</td><td>Gold was found at Coloma and miners poured in from Mexico, Chile, China, Ireland, France, Australia and the eastern states.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Camp rules</td><td>With no courts nearby, camps wrote their own rules about the size of a claim and how disputes would be settled.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>What camps built</td><td>Stores, boarding houses, newspapers, schools, and societies where people from the same homeland helped newcomers find work and lodging.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Not shared equally</td><td>A special tax was placed on miners who were not citizens, and Native Californians lost land and food sources across the gold country.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why would a mining camp write its own rules about claims?","Newcomers often found help through societies of people from their own homeland. Why would that kind of help matter so much in 1850?","The chart says the Gold Rush was not shared equally. Name two groups it treated unfairly, and say how."],key:["There were no courts or officials nearby and thousands of people were working the same riverbanks, so without agreed rules about claim size and settling disputes the camp could not function. People made the rules because no government had reached them yet. Partial: saying they needed rules with no account of the absence of government is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","A newcomer often arrived with no money, no English, and nobody to vouch for them. A society from their homeland could point them to work, lend tools, give lodging and settle arguments, which no public service existed to do. Partial: saying it was friendly with no account of what the society actually did is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Any two with a reason: miners who were not citizens were made to pay a special tax that others did not pay; Native Californians were pushed off land and lost the acorn groves, fish and game they lived on; Mexican Californian families often lost land they held under older grants. Partial: two groups named with no account of how they were treated is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Who Provides What",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing California's growth into an agricultural and industrial power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">Who did the work that built the California economy, and when.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>The work</th><th>Who did much of it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1860s</td><td>building the railroad through the Sierra Nevada</td><td>thousands of Chinese workers, cutting ledges and blasting tunnels</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1870s to 1900s</td><td>planting, picking and packing fruit and vegetables</td><td>farm workers from China, Japan, Mexico, the Philippines and the Dust Bowl states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1930s to 1940s</td><td>building aircraft and ships</td><td>workers who moved west in large numbers, including many women taking factory jobs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1960s onward</td><td>designing computers and software</td><td>engineers trained at California universities and arriving from around the world</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Pick one period and explain how that work changed what California could sell or build.","What do all four rows have in common about where the workers came from?","Why would a growing industry draw workers from far away rather than only from nearby towns?"],key:["Any one explained: the railroad let California sell crops and goods across the country; farm workers made large scale fruit and vegetable growing possible; aircraft and shipyards made California a manufacturing center; computer work made it a center for technology sold worldwide. Partial: a period named with nothing said about what California could sell or build is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","In every period much of the work was done by people who came from somewhere else, from other countries or other states. California's growth depended on newcomers. Partial: naming newcomers in one period only is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","A new industry needs many workers at once, more than a nearby town holds, and often needs particular skills. Wages and the chance of work draw people from far away, which is how each of these industries filled its jobs. Partial: saying wages were higher with nothing said about the number of workers needed is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of local, state and federal government",html:()=>`<p class="big">Services people use, and the level of government that provides each one.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Service</th><th>Level</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the city fire department</td><td>local</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the public library</td><td>local, with some state and county funding</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the state highway through town</td><td>state</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rules for public schools and much of their funding</td><td>state, with local school boards running the schools</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the post</td><td>federal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>national parks</td><td>federal</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two services in the chart that more than one level has a hand in. Then explain the split.","The park group wants a new crossing on the state highway. Which level do they ask, and who else might they need to involve?","Name one way a resident can ask a government for something. Then say which level they would be speaking to."],key:["The library, funded partly locally and partly by the county or state, and the schools, where the state sets rules and much of the funding while a local board runs them day to day. Either pair explained. Partial: two services named with no split explained is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The state, because it owns and manages the highway. They would also involve the city council, which speaks for the town and manages the streets that meet the highway, and they may need the county if the road crosses county land. Partial: naming the state with nobody else involved is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","Any real route with the level named: speak at a city council meeting, which is local; write to a state Assembly member or senator, which is state; write to a member of Congress, which is federal; sign a petition or vote in that level's election. Partial: a route named with the level of government not identified is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[2].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Quote It Exactly",std:["RI.5.1"],needs:["RI.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the article word for word, and say where in the article your quote comes from.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The writer says a rota “cannot make anyone care.” Quote the sentence that tells you what she thinks it can do instead, and explain what she means by it.</li><li>Quote accurately from the article to support this inference: the garden thinks a swap is a normal part of the system, not a failure of it. Use two quotations.</li></ol>${rotaHtml(true)}`,key:["Quotes “It only makes it easy to know what you promised, and easy to tell when you have not kept it.” She means a rota is a record, not a motive: it makes the promise visible so that both the person and everyone else can see whether it was kept. The quotation has to be exact. (RI.5.1, quoting accurately to explain the text)","Two exact quotations, such as “If the day does not work, that person finds a swap and writes it on the sheet in pencil” and “Last season the garden counted 9 swaps out of 28 Saturdays, and not one Saturday went unopened.” Together they show the garden plans for swaps, keeps a way to record them, and counts a season with 9 of them as a success. (RI.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Unlike Bottoms",std:["5.NF.A.1"],needs:["4.NF.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show the equal fractions you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>1/2 + 1/3</li><li>3/4 &minus; 2/5</li><li>Rosalind weeded 2/3 of her row on Saturday and 5/6 of another row on Sunday. How much did she weed in all? Give the answer as a mixed number.</li></ol>`,key:["5/6. Rewrite both in sixths: 1/2 = 3/6 and 1/3 = 2/6, so 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. (5.NF.A.1)","7/20. Rewrite both in twentieths: 3/4 = 15/20 and 2/5 = 8/20, so 15/20 − 8/20 = 7/20. (5.NF.A.1)","3/2, which is 1 1/2 rows. Rewrite in sixths: 2/3 = 4/6, so 4/6 + 5/6 = 9/6, and 9/6 reduces to 3/2, or 1 1/2. (5.NF.A.1)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Quote the Story",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Quote the story word for word.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Quote accurately from the story to support this inference: Dev did not think the swap cost him anything. Use two quotations.</li><li>At the end Dev says, “Neither did you.” Quote the earlier line he is answering, and explain what his answer means.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Two exact quotations, such as “Dev said yes before she finished asking” and “his whole May was a blank page,” or “he would take the first Saturday and the twelfth both if she wanted.” Together they show he agreed instantly and had nothing else planned. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately to support an inference)","He is answering “You did not have to come.” His answer means that she was the one on the rota for the twelfth and she came in the rain anyway, so neither of them was there because they had to be. The quotation has to be exact. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately to explain the text)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Down to Thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.3"],needs:["4.NF.C.7"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 0.407 ____ 0.47</li><li>Write 0.325 in expanded form, using fractions for the places after the decimal point.</li><li>Write the number two and fifty-eight hundredths in digits. Then write 4.06 in words.</li></ol>`,key:["0.407 &lt; 0.47. Line the places up: 0.470 against 0.407. The tenths match, but 7 hundredths beats 0 hundredths. (5.NBT.A.3a and 5.NBT.A.3b)","0.325 = 3 × (1/10) + 2 × (1/100) + 5 × (1/1000). Accept 0.3 + 0.02 + 0.005. (5.NBT.A.3a, expanded form)","2.58, and 4.06 is four and six hundredths. (5.NBT.A.3a, reading and writing decimals)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Two Steps of Units",std:["5.MD.A.1"],needs:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The hose is 24 meters long. The crew cuts it into sections 80 centimeters long. How many sections do they get?</li><li>A bag weighs 3.5 kilograms. How many grams is that?</li><li>A barrel holds 120 liters. A watering can holds 8 liters. How many full cans does it take to empty the barrel?</li></ol>`,key:["30 sections. First change 24 meters into centimeters: 24 × 100 = 2,400. Then 2,400 ÷ 80 = 30. (5.MD.A.1, converting units inside a multi-step problem)","3,500 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 3.5 × 1,000 = 3,500. (5.MD.A.1)","15 cans. 120 ÷ 8 = 15. Check: 15 × 8 = 120. (5.MD.A.1)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Tense and Tone",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Change this sentence so it tells about something finished before another past event: Dev waits under the eave. Start with <b>By the time she arrived, Dev</b> ____.</li><li>Fix the tense shift: “Rosalind opened the gate and walks straight to the barrels.”</li><li>Which pair of words fits? Dev took ____ the first Saturday ____ the twelfth. (<b>not only / but also</b>, or <b>either / or</b>) Pick the pair that matches this meaning: he offered to take both.</li></ol>`,key:["By the time she arrived, Dev had been waiting under the eave. Accept “had waited.” The past perfect, had plus the verb, shows the waiting was finished before she arrived. (L.5.1b and L.5.1c, verb tense)","Rosalind opened the gate and walked straight to the barrels. Both verbs now sit in the past. (L.5.1d, correcting an inappropriate shift in verb tense)","not only / but also. Dev took not only the first Saturday but also the twelfth. either / or would mean one of the two, not both. (L.5.1a, correlative conjunctions)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RL.4.4","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","RL.5.1","RI.5.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function unitHtml(){return F.table(["1 of the bigger unit","equals","of the smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 centimeter","=","10 millimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 minute","=","60 seconds"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"],["1 yard","=","3 feet"]])+`<p class="small">Going from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply. The number gets bigger because each unit is smaller.</p>`}function pvHtml(){return F.table(["Place","What one of them is worth","In the number 3,333"],[["Thousands","1,000","the first 3 is worth 3,000"],["Hundreds","100","the second 3 is worth 300"],["Tens","10","the third 3 is worth 30"],["Ones","1","the last 3 is worth 3"]])+`<p class="small">Each place to the left is worth ten times the place on its right. Each place to the right is worth one tenth as much.</p>`}function decHtml(){return F.table(["Fraction","Decimal","Said out loud"],[["7/10","0.7","seven tenths"],["70/100","0.70","seventy hundredths"],["3/100","0.03","three hundredths"],["43/100","0.43","forty-three hundredths"],["1/10","0.1","one tenth"]])+`<p class="small">To compare two decimals, line up the decimal points and compare the tenths first, then the hundredths. Writing a 0 on the end of a decimal does not change its value, so 0.6 and 0.60 are the same number.</p>`}const FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12];let fracOn={};function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}function fcardHtml(){return`<ul class="starters"><li>Equal fractions: multiply the top and the bottom by the same number. 3/4 = (3 × 3)/(4 × 3) = 9/12.</li><li>Going the other way: divide the top and the bottom by the same number. 6/10 = (6 ÷ 2)/(10 ÷ 2) = 3/5.</li><li>To compare two fractions, rewrite both with the same bottom number, then compare the tops.</li><li>Benchmark: a fraction is more than 1/2 when the top is more than half the bottom.</li><li>A comparison only works when both fractions name parts of the same whole.</li></ul>`}const STARTERS={opinion:["I think the garden should ____.","One reason is ____.","The fact box says ____.","In addition, ____.","That is why ____."],facts:["A rota is ____.","At Westmill, ____.","For example, ____.","If your Saturday does not work, ____.","Now you know ____."],story:["It was ____ when I got there.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","A few minutes later, ____.","By the end of the morning, ____."],"W.4.8":["Note: ____ (Source 1)","Note: ____ (Source 2)","My sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____."],"RI.4.9":["Source 1 says ____.","Source 2 says ____.","Put together, ____."],"SL.4.4":["I want to tell you about ____.","First, here is how it works: ____.","My part is ____.","One thing I remember is ____."],"RI.4.1":["The article says, “____.”","I can tell ____ because the article says “____” and “____.”"],"RL.4.1":["The story says, “____.”","I can tell ____ because the story says “____” and “____.”"],"RL.4.4":["The word ____ means ____.","I know because the story says “____.”","It is not really ____. It means ____."]};const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.8"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.NBT.A.1","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.OA.A.1","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","5.NF.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},units:{name:"Measurement unit card",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:unitHtml},pv:{name:"Place value card",std:["4.NBT.A.1","4.NBT.A.3","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvHtml},dec:{name:"Fraction and decimal card",std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7","5.NBT.A.3"],html:decHtml},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.NF.C.6","5.NF.A.1"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},fcard:{name:"Fraction rules card",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1"],html:fcardHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3","W.4.8","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","SL.4.4","RL.4.1","RL.4.4"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The chart shows the times tables but not the place values, so it does not answer this stop. Note that it was open."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.MD.A.1","5.NF.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on the setup, the unit, and the reasoning, not on the fact itself."},{t:"units",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The unit card gives every equivalence the stop asks for, so this stop then shows only whether the student can pick the right operation. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value card names each place and states the ten times rule, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"dec",std:["4.NF.C.6"],msg:"The card shows tenths and hundredths written both ways, so count the writing questions as supported, not independent."},{t:"dec",std:["4.NF.C.7","5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The card gives the method for comparing decimals and states that 0.6 and 0.60 are the same number, so count the comparisons as supported."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips show the piece sizes and the equal shading, so rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show rather than on the comparison itself."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.C.6"],msg:"The tenths row on the strips lines up with the number line on this stop. Note that the student used it to place the decimal."},{t:"fcard",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","5.NF.A.1"],msg:"The rules card gives the method for equal fractions, for comparing, and the half benchmark. Count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.8"],msg:"The frames show how to mark a note with its source and how to lay out the source list, so count the source list check as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.1","RL.4.1","RL.4.4"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on the accuracy of the quotation and on the thinking, not on the sentence shape."},{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The chart gives the products, so rate on whether the student sets up the comparison or picks area against perimeter, not on the fact itself."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value card names each place, which is half of a rounding question. Note that it was open."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.9"],msg:"The frames tell the student to name each source and then join the two. Rate on the content pulled from each text, not on the sentence shape."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the report, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the passage, the article, or the fact box was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
